1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Funding for this program is provided by

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Additional funding provided by

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The U.S. Department of Education

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,500
We ended last time talking about

5
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,000
the narrative conception of the self.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
We were testing the narrative conception

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,500
of the self and the idea

8
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,000
of obligations of solidarity or membership

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
that did not flow from consent

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
that claimed us for reasons unrelated

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
to a contract or an agreement

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
or a choice we may have made.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
And we were debating among ourselves whether

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
there are any obligations of this kind

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
or whether all apparent

16
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
obligations of solidarity and membership

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
can be translated into consent or reciprocity

18
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
or a universal duty that we owe persons qua persons.

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
And then there were those who defended

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
the idea of loyalty and of patriotism.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,500
So the idea of loyalty

22
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:34,000
and of solidarity and of membership

23
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
gathered a certain kind of

24
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
intuitive moral force in our discussion.

25
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
And then, as we concluded,

26
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
we considered what seems to be

27
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
a pretty powerful counter-example to that idea.

28
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Namely, the film of those

29
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
southern segregationists in the 1950s.

30
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
And they talked all about their traditions, their history,

31
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
the way in which their identities

32
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
were bound up with their life history.

33
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Do you remember that?

34
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
And what flowed from that history,

35
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,500
from that narrative sense of

36
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,000
identity for those southern segregationists?

37
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
They said we have to defend our way of life.

38
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,500
Is this a fatal or

39
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:24,000
a decisive objection to the idea

40
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
of the narrative conception of the self?

41
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
That's the question we were left with.

42
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
What I would like to do today

43
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,500
is to advance an argument and

44
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:35,000
see what you make of it.

45
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
And let me tell you what that argument is.

46
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,000
I would like to defend

47
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
the narrative conception of the person

48
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:51,000
as against the voluntrous conception.

49
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
I would like to defend

50
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,500
the idea that there are

51
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,000
obligations of solidarity or membership.

52
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Then, I want to suggest

53
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
that there being such obligations,

54
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
lends force to the idea,

55
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
when we turn to justice,

56
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
that arguments about justice

57
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
can't be detached, cannot be detached after all,

58
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
from questions of the good.

59
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
But I wanted to distinguish two different ways

60
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
in which justice might be tied to the good

61
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
and argue for one of them.

62
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Now, the voluntrous conception of

63
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
the person of Kant and Rawls,

64
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
we saw was powerful and liberating.

65
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
A further appeal is its universal aspiration.

66
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The idea of treating persons as persons,

67
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
without prejudice, without discrimination.

68
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
And I think that's what led some among us

69
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
to argue that, okay, maybe

70
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
there are obligations of membership,

71
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
but they are always subordinate,

72
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
they must always be subordinate.

73
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
To the duties that we

74
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
have to human beings as such,

75
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
the universal duties.

76
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
But is that right?

77
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
If our encompassing loyalties should always take precedence

78
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
over more particular ones,

79
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
then the distinction between friends and strangers

80
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
should ideally be overcome.

81
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Our special concern for the welfare of friends

82
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,000
would be a kind of prejudice.

83
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
A measure of our distance from universal human concern.

84
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
But if you look closely at that idea,

85
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
what kind of a moral universe,

86
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
what kind of moral imagination would that lead you to?

87
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
The enlightenment philosopher Montesquieu

88
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
gives perhaps the most powerful,

89
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
and I think ultimately the most honest account

90
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
of where this relentless,

91
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
universalizing tendency

92
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
leads the moral imagination.

93
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Here's how Montesquieu put it.

94
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,000
He said,

95
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
a truly virtuous man

96
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
would come to the aid of the most distant stranger

97
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
as quickly as to his own friend.

98
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,000
And then he adds,

99
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
listen to this,

100
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
if men were perfectly virtuous,

101
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
they wouldn't have friends.

102
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
But it's difficult to imagine a world

103
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
in which persons were so virtuous

104
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
that they had no friends,

105
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
only a universal disposition to friendliness.

106
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
The problem isn't simply

107
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
that such a world would be difficult to bring about,

108
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
that it's unrealistic.

109
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
The deeper problem is that

110
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
such a world would be difficult to recognize

111
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
as a human world.

112
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The love of humanity is a noble secret,

113
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
and it's not just a sentimental sentiment.

114
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
But most of the time,

115
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
we live our lives by smaller solidarities.

116
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
This may reflect certain limits

117
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
to the bounds of moral sympathy,

118
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
but more important,

119
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
it reflects the fact

120
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
that we learn to love humanity,

121
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
not in general,

122
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
but through its particular expressions.

123
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
So, these are some considerations.

124
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
They're not knock-down arguments.

125
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
But moral philosophy

126
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
can't offer knock-down arguments,

127
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
but considerations

128
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
of the kinds that we've been discussing

129
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
and arguing about all along.

130
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Well, suppose that's right.

131
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
One way of assessing whether this picture

132
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
of the person and of obligation is right

133
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,000
is to see what are its consequences for justice.

134
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
And here's where it confronts a serious problem.

135
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,000
And here we go back to our Southern segment,

136
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
back to our Southern segregationists.

137
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
They felt the weight of history.

138
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Do we admire their character,

139
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:13,000
these segregationists,

140
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
who wanted to preserve their way of life?

141
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Are we committed to saying,

142
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
if we accept the idea of solidarity and membership,

143
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
are we committed to saying

144
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
that justice is tied to the good

145
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
in the sense that justice means

146
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
whatever a particular community or tradition

147
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
says it means,

148
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
including those Southern segregationists?

149
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Here it's important to distinguish

150
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
two different ways

151
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
in which justice can be tied to the good.

152
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
One is a relativist way.

153
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
That's the way that says

154
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,000
to think about rights,

155
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
to think about justice,

156
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
look to the values that happen to prevail

157
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,000
in any given community

158
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
at any given time.

159
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:10,000
Don't judge them by some outside standard,

160
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
but instead conceive justice

161
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,000
as a matter of being faithful

162
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
to the shared understandings

163
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
of a particular tradition.

164
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
But there's a problem with this way

165
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
of tying justice to the good.

166
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
The problem is that it makes justice

167
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
wholly conventional,

168
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
a product of circumstance.

169
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
And this deprives justice

170
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
of its critical character.

171
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
But there is a second way

172
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
in which justice can be tied with

173
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
or bound up with the good.

174
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
On this second non-relativist way

175
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
of linking justice with conceptions of the good,

176
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
principles of justice depend

177
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:52,000
for their justification

178
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
not on the values that happen to prevail

179
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
at any given moment in a certain place,

180
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
but instead on the moral worth

181
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:00,000
or the intrinsic value

182
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
of the ends rights serve.

183
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
On this non-relativist view,

184
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
the case for recognizing a right

185
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
depends on showing that it honors

186
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
or advances some important human good.

187
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
This second way of tying justice

188
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
to the good is not,

189
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,000
strictly speaking,

190
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
communitarian,

191
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
if by communitarian you mean

192
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,000
just giving over to a particular

193
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
individual or a particular group

194
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,000
of people.

195
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,000
It's a way of tying justice

196
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,000
to the good,

197
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
or to a particular community,

198
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
the definition of justice.

199
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Now what I would like to suggest

200
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:39,000
that of these two different ways

201
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
of linking justice to the good,

202
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,000
the first is insufficient.

203
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Because the first leaves justice

204
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
the creature of convention.

205
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
It doesn't give us enough moral resources

206
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
to respond to those southern segregationists

207
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
who invoke their way of life,

208
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:58,000
their traditions,

209
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
their way of doing things.

210
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
But if justice is bound up

211
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
with the good in a non-relativist way,

212
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
there's a big challenge,

213
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
a big question to answer.

214
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
How can we reason about the good?

215
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
What about the fact that people

216
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
hold different conceptions of the good,

217
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
different ideas about the purposes

218
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
of key social institutions?

219
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Different ideas about what social goods

220
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
and human goods are worthy of honor

221
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
and recognition?

222
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
We live in a pluralist society.

223
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
People disagree about the good.

224
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:41,000
That's one of the incentives

225
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
to try to find principles of justice

226
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
and rights that don't depend

227
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
on any particular ends or purposes

228
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
or goods.

229
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
So is there a way to reason about the good?

230
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Before addressing that question,

231
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
I want to address a slightly easier question.

232
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Is it necessary, is it unavoidable

233
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
when arguing about justice

234
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
to argue about the good?

235
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
And my answer to that question is yes.

236
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:11,000
It's unavoidable.

237
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
It's necessary.

238
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
So for the remainder of today,

239
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:16,000
I want to take up,

240
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
I want to try to advance that claim.

241
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
That reasoning about the good,

242
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
about purposes and ends

243
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
is inevitable.

244
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
And that reasoning about the good

245
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
and ends is an unavoidable feature

246
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
of arguing about justice.

247
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
It's necessary.

248
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Let me see if I can establish that.

249
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
And for that, I'd like for us

250
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:42,000
to begin a discussion of same-sex marriage.

251
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Now, same-sex marriage draws on,

252
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:50,000
implicates deeply contested

253
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and controversial ideas

254
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
religiously.

255
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
And so there's a powerful incentive

256
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
to embrace a conception of justice

257
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,000
or of rights

258
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
that doesn't require the society as a whole

259
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
to pass judgment, one way or another,

260
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,000
on those hotly contested

261
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
moral and religious questions

262
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
about the moral permissibility of homosexuality,

263
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
about the proper ends of marriage

264
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
as a social institution.

265
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
So clearly, if there's an incentive

266
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
to resolve this question,

267
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:28,000
to define people's rights

268
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
in a way that doesn't require

269
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:31,000
the society as a whole

270
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
to sort out those moral and religious disputes,

271
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
that would be very attractive.

272
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
So what I would like to do now

273
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
is to see, using the same-sex marriage case,

274
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
whether it's possible

275
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
to detach one's views

276
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
about the moral permissibility of homosexuality

277
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
and about the purpose,

278
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
the end of marriage,

279
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
to detach those questions

280
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
from the question of whether the state

281
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:02,000
should recognize same-sex marriage or not.

282
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
So let's begin.

283
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
I would like to begin

284
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
by hearing the arguments of those

285
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
who believe that there should be

286
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
no same-sex marriage,

