we've just been hearing,

849
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:39,000
defended by Andrea and by Dan.

850
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:40,000
The court begins,

851
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:44,000
and this is Chief Justice Margaret Marshall's opinion,

852
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:46,000
it begins with an attempt

853
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:48,000
at liberal neutrality.

854
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:50,000
Many people hold deep-seated religious,

855
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:52,000
moral, and ethical convictions

856
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:54,000
that marriage should be limited to the union

857
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:56,000
of one man and one woman

858
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:59,000
and that homosexual conduct is immoral.

859
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:01,000
Many hold equally strong religious, moral,

860
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:02,000
and ethical convictions

861
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:05,000
that same-sex couples are entitled to be married,

862
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:08,000
that homosexual persons should be treated no differently

863
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:10,000
than their heterosexual neighbors.

864
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:11,000
This is the court.

865
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:15,000
Neither view answers the question before us.

866
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:17,000
What is at stake is, quote,

867
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:20,000
respect for individual autonomy

868
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:22,000
and equality under law.

869
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:26,000
At stake is an individual freely choosing

870
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:29,000
the person with whom to share an exclusive commitment.

871
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:32,000
In other words, at issue is not

872
00:34:32,000 --> 00:34:34,000
the moral worth of the choice

873
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:37,000
but the right of the individual to make it.

874
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:42,000
So this is the liberal neutral strand

875
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:44,000
in the court opinion,

876
00:34:44,000 --> 00:34:45,000
the voluntrous strand,

877
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:49,000
the one that emphasizes autonomy, choice, consent.

878
00:34:52,000 --> 00:34:54,000
But the court seemed to realize

879
00:34:54,000 --> 00:34:57,000
that the liberal case, the neutral case,

880
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:59,000
for recognizing same-sex marriage

881
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:01,000
doesn't succeed,

882
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:03,000
doesn't get you all the way to that position

883
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:05,000
because if it were only a matter

884
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:08,000
of respect for individual autonomy,

885
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:11,000
if government were truly neutral

886
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,000
on the moral worth

887
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:16,000
of voluntary intimate relationships,

888
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:21,000
then it should adopt a different policy,

889
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:24,000
which is to remove government

890
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:27,000
and the state altogether

891
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:30,000
from according recognition

892
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:32,000
to certain associations,

893
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:34,000
certain kinds of unions,

894
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:36,000
rather than others.

895
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:39,000
If government really must be neutral,

896
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:43,000
then the consistent position,

897
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:45,000
is what we here have been describing

898
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:47,000
as the third position,

899
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:49,000
the one defended in the article

900
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:50,000
by Michael Kinsley,

901
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:54,000
who argues for the abolition of marriage

902
00:35:54,000 --> 00:35:56,000
at least as a state function.

903
00:35:56,000 --> 00:35:58,000
Perhaps a better term for this

904
00:35:58,000 --> 00:36:01,000
is the disestablishment of religion.

905
00:36:01,000 --> 00:36:04,000
This is Kinsley's proposal.

906
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:07,000
He points out that the reason

907
00:36:07,000 --> 00:36:11,000
for the opposition to same-sex marriage

908
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:13,000
is that it would go beyond

909
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:15,000
neutral toleration

910
00:36:15,000 --> 00:36:17,000
and give same-sex marriage

911
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:19,000
a government stamp of approval.

912
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:21,000
That's at the heart of the dispute.

913
00:36:21,000 --> 00:36:24,000
In Aristotle's terms,

914
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:26,000
at issue here is the proper distribution

915
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:28,000
of offices and honors,

916
00:36:28,000 --> 00:36:31,000
a matter of social recognition.

917
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:35,000
Same-sex marriage can't be justified

918
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:37,000
on the basis of liberal neutrality

919
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:38,000
or nondiscrimination

920
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:40,000
or autonomy rights alone,

921
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:42,000
because the question at stake

922
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:44,000
in the public debate

923
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:45,000
is whether same-sex unions

924
00:36:45,000 --> 00:36:46,000
have moral worth,

925
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:48,000
whether they're worthy of honor

926
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:49,000
and recognition,

927
00:36:49,000 --> 00:36:51,000
and whether they fit the purpose

928
00:36:51,000 --> 00:36:55,000
of the social institution of marriage.

929
00:36:55,000 --> 00:36:57,000
So, Kinsley says,

930
00:36:57,000 --> 00:36:59,000
you want to be neutral?

931
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:01,000
Then let churches

932
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:03,000
and other religious institutions

933
00:37:03,000 --> 00:37:05,000
offer marriage ceremonies.

934
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:08,000
Let department stores and casinos

935
00:37:08,000 --> 00:37:10,000
get into the act if they want to.

936
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:11,000
This is Kinsley.

937
00:37:11,000 --> 00:37:13,000
Let couples celebrate their union

938
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:15,000
in any way they choose

939
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,000
and consider themselves married

940
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:19,000
whenever they want.

941
00:37:19,000 --> 00:37:21,000
And if three people want to get married,

942
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:24,000
or if one person wants to marry

943
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:27,000
himself or herself,

944
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:28,000
and someone else wants to conduct

945
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:29,000
a ceremony for them

946
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:31,000
and declare them married,

947
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:32,000
let them.

948
00:37:32,000 --> 00:37:33,000
If you and your government

949
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:34,000
are implicated,

950
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:35,000
why do you care?

951
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:37,000
This is Kinsley.

952
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:40,000
But this is not the position

953
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:42,000
that the Supreme Judicial Court

954
00:37:42,000 --> 00:37:44,000
of Massachusetts wanted.

955
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:46,000
They didn't call for the abolition

956
00:37:46,000 --> 00:37:49,000
or for the disestablishment of marriage.

957
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:51,000
The court did not question

958
00:37:51,000 --> 00:37:53,000
government's role in conferring

959
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:54,000
social recognition

960
00:37:54,000 --> 00:37:56,000
on some intimate associations

961
00:37:56,000 --> 00:37:57,000
rather than others.

962
00:37:57,000 --> 00:37:59,000
To the contrary,

963
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:01,000
the court waxes eloquent

964
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:04,000
about marriage as, quote,

965
00:38:04,000 --> 00:38:06,000
one of our community's most rewarding

966
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:08,000
and cherished institutions.

967
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:09,000
And then it goes on

968
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:11,000
to expand the definition of marriage

969
00:38:11,000 --> 00:38:14,000
to include partners of the same sex.

970
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:16,000
And in doing so,

971
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:19,000
it acknowledges that marriage

972
00:38:19,000 --> 00:38:22,000
is more than a matter

973
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:23,000
of tolerating choices

974
00:38:23,000 --> 00:38:25,000
that individuals make.

975
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:27,000
It's also a matter

976
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:31,000
of social recognition and honor.

977
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:33,000
As Justice Marshall wrote,

978
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:34,000
in a real sense,

979
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:35,000
there are three partners

980
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:37,000
to every civil marriage,

981
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:39,000
two willing spouses,

982
00:38:39,000 --> 00:38:42,000
and an approving state.

983
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:43,000
Marriage is at once

984
00:38:43,000 --> 00:38:45,000
a deeply personal commitment,

985
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:47,000
but also a highly public celebration

986
00:38:47,000 --> 00:38:49,000
of the ideals of mutuality,

987
00:38:49,000 --> 00:38:53,000
companionship, intimacy, fidelity, and family.

988
00:38:53,000 --> 00:38:55,000
This is the court.

989
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:57,000
Now, this is reaching well beyond

990
00:38:57,000 --> 00:38:59,000
liberal neutrality.

991
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:01,000
This is celebrating and affirming

992
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:03,000
marriage as an honorific,

993
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:07,000
as a form of public recognition.

994
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:08,000
And therefore, the court found

995
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:11,000
that it couldn't avoid the debate

996
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:14,000
about the telos of marriage.

997
00:39:14,000 --> 00:39:16,000
Justice Marshall's opinion

998
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:19,000
considers and rejects the notion

999
00:39:19,000 --> 00:39:21,000
that the primary purpose of marriage

1000
00:39:21,000 --> 00:39:23,000
is procreation.

1001
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:25,000
She points out that there's no requirement

1002
00:39:25,000 --> 00:39:27,000
that applicants for a marriage license

1003
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:30,000
who are heterosexuals attest to their ability

1004
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:32,000
or their intention to conceive children.

1005
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:35,000
Fertility is not a condition of marriage.

1006
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:37,000
People who cannot stir from their deathbed,

1007
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:39,000
may marry.

1008
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:41,000
So she advances all kinds of arguments

1009
00:39:41,000 --> 00:39:45,000
along the lines that we began last time

1010
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:48,000
about what the proper and the essential nature

1011
00:39:48,000 --> 00:39:50,000
of the telos of marriage is.

1012
00:39:50,000 --> 00:39:51,000
And she concludes,

1013
00:39:51,000 --> 00:39:53,000
not procreation,

1014
00:39:53,000 --> 00:39:55,000
but the exclusive and permanent commitment

1015
00:39:55,000 --> 00:39:58,000
of the partners to one another

1016
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:04,000
is the essential point and purpose of marriage.

1017
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Now, nothing I've said

1018
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:11,000
about this court opinion

1019
00:40:11,000 --> 00:40:15,000
is an argument for or against same-sex marriage,

1020
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:18,000
but it is an argument against the claim

1021
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:22,000
that you can favor or oppose same-sex marriage

1022
00:40:22,000 --> 00:40:24,000
while remaining neutral

1023
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:28,000
on the underlying moral and religious questions.

1024
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:31,000
So all of this is to suggest

1025
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:34,000
that at least in some

1026
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:36,000
of the hotly contested

1027
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:38,000
debates about justice and rights

1028
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:41,000
that we have in our society,

1029
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:43,000
the attempt to be neutral,

1030
00:40:43,000 --> 00:40:45,000
the attempt to say

1031
00:40:45,000 --> 00:40:47,000
it's just a matter of consent

1032
00:40:47,000 --> 00:40:49,000
and choice and autonomy,

1033
00:40:49,000 --> 00:40:51,000
we take no stand,

1034
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:53,000
that doesn't succeed.

1035
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:54,000
Even the court,

1036
00:40:54,000 --> 00:40:56,000
which wants to be neutral

1037
00:40:56,000 --> 00:40:58,000
on these moral and religious disputes,

1038
00:40:58,000 --> 00:41:01,000
finds that it can't.

1039
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:05,000
What then about our second question?

1040
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:08,000
If reasoning about the good

1041
00:41:08,000 --> 00:41:11,000
is unavoidable

1042
00:41:11,000 --> 00:41:15,000
in debates about justice and rights,

1043
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:18,000
is it possible?

1044
00:41:18,000 --> 00:41:21,000
If reasoning about the good

1045
00:41:21,000 --> 00:41:23,000
means that you must have

1046
00:41:23,000 --> 00:41:25,000
a single principle or rule

1047
00:41:25,000 --> 00:41:27,000
or maxim

1048
00:41:27,000 --> 00:41:29,000
or criterion

1049
00:41:29,000 --> 00:41:32,000
for the good life,

1050
00:41:32,000 --> 00:41:33,000
that you simply plug in

1051
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:34,000
every time you have

1052
00:41:34,000 --> 00:41:36,000
a disagreement about morality,

1053
00:41:36,000 --> 00:41:40,000
then the answer is no.

1054
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:44,000
But having a single principle or rule

1055
00:41:44,000 --> 00:41:46,000
is not the only way,

1056
00:41:46,000 --> 00:41:48,000
not the best way

1057
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:50,000
of reasoning

1058
00:41:50,000 --> 00:41:52,000
either about the good life

1059
00:41:52,000 --> 00:41:54,000
or about justice.

1060
00:41:54,000 --> 00:41:56,000
Think back,

1061
00:41:56,000 --> 00:41:58,000
think back to the arguments

1062
00:41:58,000 --> 00:42:00,000
that we've been having here

1063
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:03,000
about justice and about rights

1064
00:42:03,000 --> 00:42:06,000
and sometimes about the good life.

1065
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:09,000
How have those arguments proceeded?

1066
00:42:09,000 --> 00:42:11,000
They've proceeded

1067
00:42:11,000 --> 00:42:15,000
very much in the way

1068
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:18,000
that Aristotle suggests,

1069
00:42:18,000 --> 00:42:20,000
moving back and forth

1070
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:23,000
between our judgments about particulars,

1071
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:27,000
particular cases, events, stories, questions,

1072
00:42:27,000 --> 00:42:29,000
back and forth between our judgments

1073
00:42:29,000 --> 00:42:31,000
about particular cases

1074
00:42:31,000 --> 00:42:33,000
and more general principles

1075
00:42:33,000 --> 00:42:35,000
that make sense of our reasons

1076
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:36,000
for the positions we take

1077
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:38,000
on the particular cases.

1078
00:42:38,000 --> 00:42:42,000
This dialectical way

1079
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:44,000
of doing moral reasoning

1080
00:42:44,000 --> 00:42:46,000
goes back to the ancients,

1081
00:42:46,000 --> 00:42:48,000
to Plato and Aristotle,

1082
00:42:48,000 --> 00:42:51,000
but it doesn't stop with them

1083
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:53,000
because there is a version

1084
00:42:53,000 --> 00:42:56,000
of Socratic or dialectical moral reasoning

1085
00:42:56,000 --> 00:42:58,000
that is defended

1086
00:42:58,000 --> 00:43:01,000
with great clarity and force

1087
00:43:01,000 --> 00:43:02,000
by John Rawls.

1088
00:43:03,000 --> 00:43:05,000
In giving an account

1089
00:43:05,000 --> 00:43:08,000
of his method of justifying

1090
00:43:08,000 --> 00:43:10,000
a theory of justice.

1091
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:11,000
You remember,

1092
00:43:11,000 --> 00:43:13,000
it's not only the veil of ignorance

1093
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:14,000
and the principles

1094
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:16,000
that Rawls argues for.

1095
00:43:16,000 --> 00:43:20,000
It's also a method of moral reasoning,

1096
00:43:20,000 --> 00:43:22,000
reasoning about justice,

1097
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:25,000
that he calls reflective equilibrium.

1098
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:26,000
What is the method

1099
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:28,000
of reflective equilibrium?

1100
00:43:28,000 --> 00:43:31,000
It's moving back and forth

1101
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:33,000
between our consistent

1102
00:43:33,000 --> 00:43:35,000
and consistent judgments

1103
00:43:35,000 --> 00:43:37,000
and our consistent judgments.

1104
00:43:37,000 --> 00:43:39,000
So, in the case of the law of law,

1105
00:43:39,000 --> 00:43:40,000
we consider judgments

1106
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:42,000
about particular cases

1107
00:43:42,000 --> 00:43:44,000
and the general principles

1108
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:46,000
we would articulate

1109
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:48,000
to make sense of those judgments.

1110
00:43:48,000 --> 00:43:50,000
And not just stopping there,

1111
00:43:50,000 --> 00:43:51,000
because we might be wrong

1112
00:43:51,000 --> 00:43:53,000
in our initial intuitions.

1113
00:43:53,000 --> 00:43:54,000
Not stopping there,

1114
00:43:54,000 --> 00:43:56,000
but then sometimes revising

1115
00:43:56,000 --> 00:43:58,000
our particular judgments

1116
00:43:58,000 --> 00:44:00,000
in the light of the principles

1117
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:02,000
once we work them out.

1118
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:04,000
And in particular cases.

1119
00:44:04,000 --> 00:44:06,000
The general point is this,

1120
00:44:06,000 --> 00:44:08,000
and here I quote Rawls,

1121
00:44:08,000 --> 00:44:10,000
a conception of justice

1122
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:11,000
can't be deduced

1123
00:44:11,000 --> 00:44:14,000
from self-evident premises.

1124
00:44:14,000 --> 00:44:15,000
It's justification

1125
00:44:15,000 --> 00:44:17,000
is a matter of the mutual support

1126
00:44:17,000 --> 00:44:19,000
of many considerations,

1127
00:44:19,000 --> 00:44:21,000
of everything fitting together

1128
00:44:21,000 --> 00:44:25,000
into one coherent view.

1129
00:44:25,000 --> 00:44:26,000
And later in a theory of justice,

1130
00:44:26,000 --> 00:44:27,000
he writes,

1131
00:44:27,000 --> 00:44:30,000
moral philosophy is Socratic.

1132
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:31,000
We may want to change

1133
00:44:31,000 --> 00:44:33,000
our present considered judgments

1134
00:44:33,000 --> 00:44:36,000
once their regulative principles

1135
00:44:36,000 --> 00:44:38,000
are brought to light.

1136
00:44:38,000 --> 00:44:42,000
Well, if Rawls accepts that idea

1137
00:44:42,000 --> 00:44:45,000
and advances that notion

1138
00:44:45,000 --> 00:44:46,000
of reflective equilibrium,

1139
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:49,000
the question we're left with is,

1140
00:44:49,000 --> 00:44:51,000
he applies that

1141
00:44:51,000 --> 00:44:53,000
to questions of justice.

1142
00:44:53,000 --> 00:44:57,000
Not to questions of morality

1143
00:44:57,000 --> 00:44:59,000
and the good life.

1144
00:44:59,000 --> 00:45:01,000
But, and that's why

1145
00:45:01,000 --> 00:45:03,000
he remains committed

1146
00:45:03,000 --> 00:45:04,000
to the priority

1147
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:06,000
of the right over the good.

1148
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:07,000
He thinks the method

1149
00:45:07,000 --> 00:45:09,000
of reflective equilibrium

1150
00:45:09,000 --> 00:45:12,000
can generate shared judgments

1151
00:45:12,000 --> 00:45:14,000
about justice and the right,

1152
00:45:14,000 --> 00:45:16,000
but he doesn't think

1153
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:18,000
they can generate shared judgments

1154
00:45:18,000 --> 00:45:20,000
about the good life,

1155
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:22,000
about what he calls comprehensive

1156
00:45:22,000 --> 00:45:25,000
moral and religious questions.

1157
00:45:25,000 --> 00:45:27,000
And the reason he thinks that

1158
00:45:27,000 --> 00:45:28,000
is that he says that

1159
00:45:28,000 --> 00:45:30,000
in modern societies

1160
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:32,000
there is a fact of reasonable

1161
00:45:32,000 --> 00:45:35,000
pluralism about the good.

1162
00:45:35,000 --> 00:45:37,000
Even conscientious people

1163
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:39,000
who reason well

1164
00:45:39,000 --> 00:45:41,000
will find that they disagree

1165
00:45:41,000 --> 00:45:44,000
about questions of the good life,

1166
00:45:44,000 --> 00:45:47,000
about morality and religion.

1167
00:45:47,000 --> 00:45:50,000
And Rawls is likely

1168
00:45:50,000 --> 00:45:51,000
right about that.

1169
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:52,000
He's not talking about

1170
00:45:52,000 --> 00:45:54,000
the fact of disagreement

1171
00:45:54,000 --> 00:45:56,000
in pluralist societies.

1172
00:45:56,000 --> 00:45:58,000
He's also suggesting

1173
00:45:58,000 --> 00:45:59,000
that there may be persisting,

1174
00:45:59,000 --> 00:46:01,000
disagreeing,

1175
00:46:01,000 --> 00:46:02,000
disagreements

1176
00:46:02,000 --> 00:46:04,000
about the good life

1177
00:46:04,000 --> 00:46:05,000
and about moral

1178
00:46:05,000 --> 00:46:08,000
and religious questions.

1179
00:46:08,000 --> 00:46:11,000
But if that's true,

1180
00:46:11,000 --> 00:46:14,000
then is he warranted

1181
00:46:14,000 --> 00:46:17,000
in his further claim

1182
00:46:17,000 --> 00:46:19,000
that the same can't be said

1183
00:46:19,000 --> 00:46:21,000
about justice?

1184
00:46:21,000 --> 00:46:23,000
Isn't it also true

1185
00:46:23,000 --> 00:46:24,000
not only that we,

1186
00:46:24,000 --> 00:46:25,000
as a matter of fact,

1187
00:46:25,000 --> 00:46:27,000
disagree about justice

1188
00:46:27,000 --> 00:46:29,000
in pluralist societies,

1189
00:46:29,000 --> 00:46:30,000
but that at least

1190
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:31,000
some of those disagreements

1191
00:46:31,000 --> 00:46:35,000
are reasonable disagreements

1192
00:46:35,000 --> 00:46:37,000
in the same way?

1193
00:46:37,000 --> 00:46:39,000
Some people favor

1194
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:42,000
a libertarian theory of justice.

1195
00:46:42,000 --> 00:46:43,000
Others,

1196
00:46:43,000 --> 00:46:45,000
a more egalitarian theory of justice.

1197
00:46:45,000 --> 00:46:47,000
And they argue.

1198
00:46:47,000 --> 00:46:49,000
And there is pluralism

1199
00:46:49,000 --> 00:46:50,000
in our society

1200
00:46:50,000 --> 00:46:51,000
as between free market,

1201
00:46:51,000 --> 00:46:52,000
laissez-faire,

1202
00:46:52,000 --> 00:46:54,000
libertarian theories of justice

1203
00:46:54,000 --> 00:46:57,000
and more egalitarian ones.

1204
00:46:57,000 --> 00:46:59,000
Is there any reason,

1205
00:46:59,000 --> 00:47:00,000
is there any difference

1206
00:47:00,000 --> 00:47:01,000
in principle

1207
00:47:01,000 --> 00:47:02,000
between the kind

1208
00:47:02,000 --> 00:47:03,000
of moral reasoning

1209
00:47:03,000 --> 00:47:05,000
and the kind of disagreements

1210
00:47:05,000 --> 00:47:06,000
that arise when we debate

1211
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:08,000
about justice

1212
00:47:08,000 --> 00:47:10,000
and the meaning of free speech

1213
00:47:10,000 --> 00:47:11,000
and the nature

1214
00:47:11,000 --> 00:47:13,000
of religious liberty?

1215
00:47:13,000 --> 00:47:16,000
Look at the debates we have

1216
00:47:16,000 --> 00:47:18,000
over appointees

1217
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:19,000
to the Supreme Court.

1218
00:47:19,000 --> 00:47:21,000
These are all disagreements

1219
00:47:21,000 --> 00:47:24,000
about justice and rights.

1220
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:26,000
Is there any difference

1221
00:47:26,000 --> 00:47:27,000
between the fact

1222
00:47:27,000 --> 00:47:29,000
of reasonable pluralism

1223
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:32,000
in the case of justice and rights

1224
00:47:32,000 --> 00:47:36,000
and in the case of morality and religion?

1225
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:37,000
In principle,

1226
00:47:37,000 --> 00:47:39,000
I don't think that there is.

1227
00:47:39,000 --> 00:47:41,000
In both cases,

1228
00:47:41,000 --> 00:47:43,000
what we do when we disagree

1229
00:47:43,000 --> 00:47:46,000
is we engage with our interlocutor,

1230
00:47:46,000 --> 00:47:48,000
as we've been doing here

1231
00:47:48,000 --> 00:47:49,000
for an entire semester.

1232
00:47:49,000 --> 00:47:51,000
We consider the arguments

1233
00:47:51,000 --> 00:47:54,000
that are provoked by particular cases.

1234
00:47:54,000 --> 00:47:56,000
We try to develop the reasons

1235
00:47:56,000 --> 00:47:57,000
that lead us to go one way

1236
00:47:57,000 --> 00:47:58,000
rather than another.

1237
00:47:58,000 --> 00:47:59,000
And then we listen

1238
00:47:59,000 --> 00:48:01,000
to the reasons of other people.

1239
00:48:01,000 --> 00:48:03,000
And sometimes we're persuaded

1240
00:48:03,000 --> 00:48:05,000
to revise our view.

1241
00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:06,000
Other times we're challenged

1242
00:48:06,000 --> 00:48:08,000
at least to shore up

1243
00:48:08,000 --> 00:48:13,000
and strengthen our view.

1244
00:48:13,000 --> 00:48:17,000
But this is how moral argument proceeds,

1245
00:48:17,000 --> 00:48:18,000
with justice,

1246
00:48:18,000 --> 00:48:21,000
and so it seems to me also,

1247
00:48:21,000 --> 00:48:25,000
with questions of the good life.

1248
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:28,000
Now, there remains a question

1249
00:48:28,000 --> 00:48:31,000
of what is the moral argument?

1250
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:34,000
And it's a further worry,

1251
00:48:34,000 --> 00:48:37,000
and it's a liberal worry.

1252
00:48:37,000 --> 00:48:40,000
What about if we're going to think

1253
00:48:40,000 --> 00:48:41,000
of our disagreements

1254
00:48:41,000 --> 00:48:44,000
about morality and religion

1255
00:48:44,000 --> 00:48:46,000
as bound up with our disagreements

1256
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:47,000
about justice?

1257
00:48:47,000 --> 00:48:49,000
How are we ever going to find

1258
00:48:49,000 --> 00:48:52,000
our way to a society

1259
00:48:52,000 --> 00:48:56,000
that accords respect

1260
00:48:56,000 --> 00:48:57,000
to fellow citizens

1261
00:48:57,000 --> 00:49:00,000
and to the people we love?

1262
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:02,000
It depends, I think,

1263
00:49:02,000 --> 00:49:04,000
on which conception

1264
00:49:04,000 --> 00:49:08,000
of respect one accepts.

1265
00:49:08,000 --> 00:49:10,000
On the liberal conception,

1266
00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:12,000
to respect our fellow citizens'

1267
00:49:12,000 --> 00:49:15,000
moral and religious convictions

1268
00:49:15,000 --> 00:49:16,000
is, so to speak,

1269
00:49:16,000 --> 00:49:17,000
to ignore them

1270
00:49:17,000 --> 00:49:19,000
for political purposes,

1271
00:49:19,000 --> 00:49:21,000
to rise above or abstract from

1272
00:49:21,000 --> 00:49:23,000
or to set aside

1273
00:49:23,000 --> 00:49:25,000
those moral and religious convictions,

1274
00:49:25,000 --> 00:49:27,000
to leave them undisturbed,

1275
00:49:27,000 --> 00:49:29,000
or to carry on our political debate

1276
00:49:29,000 --> 00:49:31,000
without reference to them.

1277
00:49:31,000 --> 00:49:34,000
But that isn't the only way,

1278
00:49:34,000 --> 00:49:38,000
or perhaps even the most plausible way

1279
00:49:38,000 --> 00:49:42,000
of understanding the mutual respect

1280
00:49:42,000 --> 00:49:46,000
on which democratic life depends.

1281
00:49:46,000 --> 00:49:48,000
There is a different conception

1282
00:49:48,000 --> 00:49:49,000
of respect,

1283
00:49:49,000 --> 00:49:51,000
according to which we respect

1284
00:49:51,000 --> 00:49:53,000
our fellow citizens' moral

1285
00:49:53,000 --> 00:49:55,000
and religious convictions,

1286
00:49:55,000 --> 00:49:56,000
not by ignoring,

1287
00:49:56,000 --> 00:49:58,000
but by engaging them,

1288
00:49:58,000 --> 00:50:00,000
by attending to them,

1289
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:02,000
sometimes by challenging

1290
00:50:02,000 --> 00:50:04,000
and contesting them,

1291
00:50:04,000 --> 00:50:07,000
sometimes by listening

1292
00:50:07,000 --> 00:50:09,000
and learning from them.

1293
00:50:09,000 --> 00:50:11,000
Now, there is no guarantee

1294
00:50:11,000 --> 00:50:13,000
that a politics of moral

1295
00:50:13,000 --> 00:50:15,000
and religious attention

1296
00:50:15,000 --> 00:50:17,000
and engagement

1297
00:50:17,000 --> 00:50:19,000
will lead in any given case

1298
00:50:19,000 --> 00:50:21,000
to agreement.

1299
00:50:21,000 --> 00:50:22,000
There is no guarantee

1300
00:50:22,000 --> 00:50:24,000
it will lead even to appreciation

1301
00:50:24,000 --> 00:50:26,000
for the moral and religious convictions

1302
00:50:26,000 --> 00:50:27,000
of others.

1303
00:50:27,000 --> 00:50:28,000
It's always possible,

1304
00:50:28,000 --> 00:50:29,000
after all,

1305
00:50:29,000 --> 00:50:31,000
that learning more about a religious

1306
00:50:31,000 --> 00:50:32,000
or a moral doctrine

1307
00:50:32,000 --> 00:50:36,000
will lead us to like it less.

1308
00:50:36,000 --> 00:50:39,000
But the respect of deliberation

1309
00:50:39,000 --> 00:50:41,000
and engagement

1310
00:50:41,000 --> 00:50:43,000
seems to me a more adequate,

1311
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:44,000
more suitable ideal

1312
00:50:44,000 --> 00:50:46,000
for a pluralist society,

1313
00:50:46,000 --> 00:50:48,000
and to the extent

1314
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:50,000
that our moral and religious disagreements

1315
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:53,000
reflect some ultimate plurality

1316
00:50:53,000 --> 00:50:55,000
of human goods,

1317
00:50:55,000 --> 00:50:58,000
a politics of moral engagement

1318
00:50:58,000 --> 00:51:00,000
will better enable us,

1319
00:51:00,000 --> 00:51:01,000
so it seems to me,

1320
00:51:01,000 --> 00:51:03,000
to appreciate

1321
00:51:03,000 --> 00:51:05,000
the distinctive goods

1322
00:51:05,000 --> 00:51:09,000
our different lives express.

1323
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:11,000
When we first came together

1324
00:51:11,000 --> 00:51:14,000
some 13 weeks ago,

1325
00:51:14,000 --> 00:51:15,000
I spoke of the exhilaration

1326
00:51:15,000 --> 00:51:17,000
of political philosophy

1327
00:51:17,000 --> 00:51:20,000
and also of its dangers,

1328
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:23,000
about how philosophy works

1329
00:51:23,000 --> 00:51:25,000
and has always worked

1330
00:51:25,000 --> 00:51:28,000
by estranging us

1331
00:51:28,000 --> 00:51:30,000
from the familiar,

1332
00:51:30,000 --> 00:51:32,000
by unsettling

1333
00:51:32,000 --> 00:51:35,000
our settled assumptions.

1334
00:51:35,000 --> 00:51:37,000
And I tried to warn you

1335
00:51:37,000 --> 00:51:41,000
that once the familiar turns strange,

1336
00:51:41,000 --> 00:51:42,000
once we begin to reflect

1337
00:51:42,000 --> 00:51:45,000
on our circumstance,

1338
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:49,000
it's never quite the same again.

1339
00:51:49,000 --> 00:51:51,000
I hope you have by now experienced

1340
00:51:51,000 --> 00:51:54,000
at least a little of this unease,

1341
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:56,000
because this is the tension

1342
00:51:56,000 --> 00:51:58,000
that animates critical reflection

1343
00:51:58,000 --> 00:52:00,000
and political improvement,

1344
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:04,000
and maybe even the moral life as well.

1345
00:52:04,000 --> 00:52:05,500
And so our argument comes

1346
00:52:05,500 --> 00:52:07,000
to an end in a sense,

1347
00:52:07,000 --> 00:52:10,000
but in another sense goes on.

1348
00:52:10,000 --> 00:52:13,000
Why, we asked at the outset,

1349
00:52:13,000 --> 00:52:16,000
why do these arguments keep going

1350
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:18,000
even if they raise questions

1351
00:52:18,000 --> 00:52:22,000
that are impossible ever finally to resolve?

1352
00:52:22,000 --> 00:52:24,000
The reason,

1353
00:52:24,000 --> 00:52:26,000
is that we live some answer

1354
00:52:26,000 --> 00:52:29,000
to these questions all the time.

1355
00:52:29,000 --> 00:52:31,000
In our public life

1356
00:52:31,000 --> 00:52:33,000
and in our personal lives,

1357
00:52:33,000 --> 00:52:36,000
philosophy is inescapable

1358
00:52:36,000 --> 00:52:41,000
even if it sometimes seems impossible.

1359
00:52:41,000 --> 00:52:44,000
We began with the thought of Kant

1360
00:52:44,000 --> 00:52:46,000
that skepticism is a resting place

1361
00:52:46,000 --> 00:52:48,000
for human reason,

1362
00:52:48,000 --> 00:52:49,000
where it can reflect upon

1363
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:52,000
its dogmatic wanderings,

1364
00:52:52,000 --> 00:52:54,000
but it is no dwelling place

1365
00:52:54,000 --> 00:52:57,000
for permanent settlement.

1366
00:52:57,000 --> 00:52:59,000
To allow ourselves simply to acquiesce

1367
00:52:59,000 --> 00:53:01,000
in skepticism or in complacence,

1368
00:53:01,000 --> 00:53:03,000
Kant wrote,

1369
00:53:03,000 --> 00:53:05,000
can never suffice

1370
00:53:05,000 --> 00:53:08,000
to overcome the restlessness of reason.

1371
00:53:08,000 --> 00:53:10,000
The aim of this course

1372
00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:12,000
has been to awaken

1373
00:53:12,000 --> 00:53:14,000
the restlessness of reason

1374
00:53:14,000 --> 00:53:16,000
and to see where it might lead.

1375
00:53:16,000 --> 00:53:19,000
And if we have done at least that,

1376
00:53:19,000 --> 00:53:21,000
and if the restlessness

1377
00:53:21,000 --> 00:53:23,000
continues to afflict you

1378
00:53:23,000 --> 00:53:26,000
in the days and years to come,

1379
00:53:26,000 --> 00:53:28,000
then we together have achieved

1380
00:53:28,000 --> 00:53:30,000
no small thing.

1381
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:31,000
Thank you.

1382
00:53:53,000 --> 00:53:54,000
Thank you.

1383
00:54:24,000 --> 00:54:25,000
Thank you.

1384
00:54:51,000 --> 00:54:52,000
